# Study 3 P0-R1 generation-3 narrow historical exception.
# p0_r1_model_execution.py was committed with CRLF endings before the rule
# above existed, and it is a bound, published, immutable generation-1 object:
# its exact bytes are hashed into p0_r1_execution_lock.json and both later
# locks. The eol=lf rule above therefore made a fresh Linux checkout report it
# as modified even when its raw worktree bytes equal its committed blob, and
# any `git add -A` would silently renormalize a protected historical file.
# `-text` disables all conversion for this one path, so the CRLF blob is
# preserved byte-for-byte and every platform checks out clean. This changes no
# committed byte: the blob is and remains 10,229 bytes, sha256
# 392f78466ee61ed303b5cf4b1fba4423e38b128441aeec1de119315b2e52a5ee.
studies/study3/pilot/p0_r1/execution/p0_r1_model_execution.py -text
tests/test_study3_p0_r1_generation3_execution_closure.py text eol=lf
# Study 3 P0-R2 generation-1 infrastructure successor.
# Registered for exactly the reason recorded in the P0-R1 block above, and
# because that omission was not theoretical here: the first generation-1
# canary run failed with `exec /usr/local/bin/p0_r2_canary_v1.sh: no such
# file or directory` because the entry points had been authored on Windows
# and committed with CRLF, so the Linux kernel read the shebang as
# `/bin/sh\r`. The image-to-Git audit passed at the same time, correctly:
# the image carried exactly the bytes Git held. Only the bytes were wrong.
# Pinning these paths to LF keeps the entry points executable on the agent
# and keeps a fresh Linux checkout byte-identical to the executable-code
# binding recorded in the P0-R2 lock.
